audience: |
  An AI coding agent that will use this answer to safely rework what identifies a remote
  machine in Coolify - the record, its number, its handle, its display name and its address -
  given that after the rework a machine can be renamed, re-addressed, re-numbered and stood in
  for by a different record meaning the same physical box. Score for that agent, not a human
  reader and not a docs page.

  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, how
  identity works on the contract itself: which fields identify a machine and where they are
  declared, cast or normalised; what the type mixes in and what hangs off it, with the
  direction of each relationship called correctly; where a lookup by identity is served out of
  a per-request cache rather than the store, and what empties that cache and when; and what
  the removal path takes, hands off and leaves behind. Second, and this is the part the ticket
  is paid for: every place in the product where a piece of a machine's identity was taken off
  the machine and put somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to, so that the code
  there holds only the piece and trusts it to still resolve later.

  The agent cannot act on a name. Every claim is worth what its location is worth: a
  file:line, and for a routine the name it is defined under. A place named without a line is a
  lead, not an answer, and it is scored as one. A place given with a line but with no reading
  of how long the kept piece outlives its machine, and no reading of what starts going wrong
  when it stops resolving, is half an answer - the agent still has to open the file and make
  the judgement itself, which is the work it was asking for.

  Two failure modes are specific to this ticket and both are worse than a short answer.
  Reciting the code that is HANDED a machine and works with it while it has it in hand: that
  code is fine after the rework, it is the largest and easiest population in the product, and
  a section of it padding out the answer is not coverage, it is the discriminator being
  avoided. And summarising a group instead of listing it - "several jobs carry the number" -
  which reads as a group with nothing in it, because the agent cannot open a summary.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    criteria:
      map_quality:
        weight: 0.4
        question: |
          This step is bounded ON PURPOSE and it is scored for being bounded. Four jobs, and
          they are not weighted alike.

          The subject: is exactly one type named as the thing that stands for one of these
          machines, and is it located? Everything after this hangs off getting that right.

          Identity, which is the half that carries: are the fields that identify a machine
          named and located at the line where each is declared, cast or normalised? Is the
          per-request cache that serves lookups by identity found, together with what empties
          it and when it is emptied? Are those connected into one account of how identity is
          established and resolved, rather than listed as places a name is mentioned?

          The composition: are the shared behaviour the type mixes in and the records it
          declares a relationship to laid out as a located list - and, crucially, is what it
          pulls in for its own use separated from what hangs off it and exists only because it
          does? That direction is what makes the second step tractable, and getting it
          backwards is a real error rather than a presentation choice.

          The removal path: is what it takes, what it hands off and what it LEAVES BEHIND each
          named? An answer reporting that the path looked complete has not done this job.

          Finally, penalise reach. The step says this is the contract and not its reach; an
          answer that goes on to enumerate the places that USE the type is spending the
          discriminator's budget here and is a worse answer than a correct short one.
      specificity:
        weight: 0.25
        question: |
          Does every field, declaration and routine carry an exact file:line AND, where it is a
          routine, the name it is defined under? A bare filename is not specific. A
          relationship or a mixed-in behaviour asserted from the shape of the class, without
          the line where it is declared, is not specific. Where the answer claims a lookup is
          served from a per-request cache, is the line that does the caching located, and the
          line that empties it, rather than inferred from a name? Where it claims something is
          left behind by the removal path, is the claim anchored to the lines of the path
          itself?
      justification:
        weight: 0.2
        question: |
          Does each located part carry a phrase on what it contributes - what it sets, what it
          caches and on what key, what empties it, what the removal path hands off? Does the
          closing sentence say what a rework of identity would have to PRESERVE for this type
          to keep meaning what it means today, rather than restating the names of the parts?
          Do NOT penalise a composition list offered without commentary: the step asks for the
          direction, not an essay.
      uncertainty:
        weight: 0.15
        question: |
          Relation accessors, model event hooks and cached lookups invite confident guessing
          from framework convention alone. Does the answer say which parts it actually opened,
          against what it is taking on the strength of a name or a familiar convention? Does it
          name what it could not settle rather than smoothing it over - in particular, is a
          claim about what the removal path leaves behind offered as something it checked, or
          asserted?

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    criteria:
      map_quality:
        weight: 0.4
        question: |
          This is the discriminator and coverage is what it measures. How many DISTINCT places
          are found where a piece of a machine's identity was taken off the machine and put
          somewhere the machine itself cannot be carried to - a column on a record about
          something else, a row of a join table, a screen's own saved state, an argument to
          work that runs later or out of process, a path or a name a system outside this
          product then owns, a bare literal standing for one particular machine?

          Score breadth across the tree, not depth in one corner. Several places inside one
          area, all reached by the same move, is one finding presented as many. Places spread
          across areas that do not resemble each other is the answer this ticket wants,
          because it is the shape a rework actually has to survive.

          Grade the exclusion too, in both directions. Code that is handed a machine and works
          with it while it has it in hand is the GROUND and is out; a section of it does not
          earn coverage and, if it is presented as the answer, the step has been failed
          regardless of how much of it is there. But a place wrongly ruled out as ground when
          the machine is in fact gone from its hands is a miss, not a judgement call.

          An answer that stops at the areas whose own subject is a machine has found the easy
          half. The places that decide this step sit in files about something else entirely.
      specificity:
        weight: 0.25
        question: |
          Does every place carry the exact file:line where the piece is taken, kept or read
          back - the line itself, not the head of the routine that contains it - together with
          the name of the routine that line sits in? A file with no line is a lead. A line
          pointing at a declaration when the taking happens elsewhere in the file is wrong,
          not approximate. Is the piece itself named - which identifying field was lifted -
          rather than gestured at?
      justification:
        weight: 0.2
        question: |
          Each place was asked for three things and only three. Is the LIFETIME given - how far
          the kept piece outlives the machine it came from, whether inside one request, inside
          one stored record, or outside this product entirely? Is the FAILURE given - one
          phrase on what starts going wrong there the first time the piece no longer resolves
          to the machine it came from? A location with neither is half an answer. Reward the
          two readings being specific to the place; penalise a single generic sentence
          repeated under every entry, and penalise prose that goes beyond the three parts,
          because the budget it spends is coverage the step did not get.
      uncertainty:
        weight: 0.15
        question: |
          The step says explicitly not to filter by confidence: a place the answer is unsure
          about is listed with the doubt written beside it, never dropped. Is that what
          happened, or is this a short list of the confident ones with the rest unaccounted
          for? Does the answer distinguish what it opened and read from what it is taking on
          the strength of a name? If it ran out of time, does it say where it stopped and what
          was still ahead of it, rather than closing on what it has as if that were the whole
          set?
